<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian Enlightenment</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Illuminismo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> was the eighteenth-century movement of reform-minded thinkers in the Italian states, centred especially on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-verri">Pietro Verri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-genovesi">Antonio Genovesi</a> and Gaetano Filangieri applied reason to economics, law and government, attacking torture, feudal privilege and clerical power. Their ideas informed the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and Tuscany and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> Naples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-enlightenment.jpg" alt="Italian Enlightenment" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Perego · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riunione_dell%27Accademia_dei_Pugni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
